              "question": "Unpolarised light is incident from the air on a plane surface of a material of refractive index <span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\mu.\\)</span>&nbsp;At a particular angle of incidence <span class=\"math-tex\">\\(i ,\\)</span>&nbsp;it is found that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other. Which of the following options is correct for this situation?\r\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td>the reflected light is polarised with its electric vector parallel to the plane of incidence.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>the reflected light is polarised with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi></math><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(i = \\text{sin}^{-1}\\dfrac{1}{\\mu}\\)</span><mfenced><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi></mfrac></mfenced></td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(i = \\text{tan}^{-1}\\dfrac{1}{\\mu}\\)</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi></math></td></tr></tbody></table>",
